# nr-office-comrs-host-containment: integration fragment

New files (this task, already in the tree): crates/mixdog-office-com/src/host/ledger.rs (the host's record of its
application), src/sweep.rs (the sweep of what a dead host left, and `Sweeper`, which runs it in the background),
tests/containment.rs, tests/containment_live.rs. No protocol method, service or SERVICE_PENDING name belongs to this task.

Nothing below is a hot file except Cargo.toml of the workspace, which this task does not touch: the files named are the
earlier tasks' files (the transport, host-runtime, route) and mixdog-exthost's containment.rs, which this task does not own,
so the exact edits are given here for the lead. Apply them in this order; every edit is small.

## 1. mixdog-exthost: the worker's job lets a child break away (crates/mixdog-exthost/src/containment.rs)

Why: the Office hosts are grandchildren of the ext-host worker, inside its kill-on-close job. A worker crash would kill a
host before its stdin's EOF cleanup and leave a hidden WINWORD/EXCEL/POWERPNT (started by DCOM, outside the job) with the
user's file open. Node started the hosts unref'd, so they outlived Node and ran that cleanup; with breakaway they outlive
the worker the same way.

In `mod os` (Windows), add to the imports, beside JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE:

    use windows_sys::Win32::System::JobObjects::JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_BREAKAWAY_OK;

and in `attach`, replace

    info.BasicLimitInformation.LimitFlags = JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE;

with

    // BREAKAWAY_OK: a process the worker starts with CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB (the Office COM hosts) is not in the job, so
    // a crash of the worker does not kill it before its EOF cleanup. Nothing else starts with that flag.
    info.BasicLimitInformation.LimitFlags = JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE | JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_BREAKAWAY_OK;

Also extend the module doc of that file by one sentence: "A child that asks to break away (an Office COM host) leaves the
job and survives its worker." No other line of containment.rs changes.

## 2. mixdog-office-com/Cargo.toml

After the existing `[target."cfg(windows)".dependencies]` table add:

    [target."cfg(windows)".dev-dependencies]
    # tests/containment_live.rs: the worker stand-in's job object (the features are the ones mixdog-exthost already uses).
    windows-sys = { workspace = true, features = ["Win32_Foundation", "Win32_Security", "Win32_System_JobObjects", "Win32_System_Threading"] }

and, after the `[[test]] name = "host_protocol"` entry, add:

    [[test]]
    name = "containment"
    path = "tests/containment.rs"

    [[test]]
    name = "containment_live"
    path = "tests/containment_live.rs"

(The workspace-hack manifest needs no change: these windows-sys features are already unified by mixdog-exthost.)

## 3. mixdog-office-com/src/lib.rs

(a) Beside `pub mod platform;` add:

    pub mod sweep;

and beside `use session::Sessions;` add:

    use sweep::Sweeper;

(b) `struct Inner` gains a field:

    sweeper: Sweeper,

(c) `OfficeCom::new` becomes:

    pub fn new(config: OfficeComConfig) -> Self {
        let sweeper = Sweeper::new(config.tokens, config.data_dir.clone());
        // Office left behind by a host that died before its cleanup (in an earlier run, or while nothing was watching).
        sweeper.request();
        Self { inner: Arc::new(Inner { config, sessions: Arc::new(Sessions::default()), sweeper }) }
    }

(d) In `open_session`, the `Attach { ... }` literal gains `sweeper: self.inner.sweeper.clone(),`.

(e) In `call_once` (impl Inner), `oneshot::attach(running, self.config.drain_grace)` becomes
`oneshot::attach(running, self.config.drain_grace, self.sweeper.clone())`.

(f) In `Inner::start`, the Rust host's arguments become
`route::rust_host_args(session, &self.config.data_dir)` (the host needs the data directory for its record).

## 4. mixdog-office-com/src/oneshot.rs

`attach` becomes

    pub(crate) fn attach(running: crate::session::Running, grace: Duration, sweeper: crate::sweep::Sweeper) -> std::sync::Arc<HostClient> {
        HostClient::attach(
            running,
            Attach { session_id: String::new(), one_shot: true, sessions: std::sync::Weak::new(), grace, sweeper },
        )
    }

## 5. mixdog-office-com/src/route.rs

`rust_host_args` takes the data directory and passes it as `--data-dir <dir>` after the host argument and the session flag:

    /// The argument that carries the data directory the host keeps its record of the application under.
    const DATA_DIR_ARG: &str = crate::host::ledger::DATA_DIR_FLAG;

    pub(crate) fn rust_host_args(session: bool, data_dir: &std::path::Path) -> Vec<OsString> {
        let mut args = vec![OsString::from(HOST_ARG)];
        if session {
            args.push(OsString::from(SESSION_ARG));
        }
        args.push(OsString::from(DATA_DIR_ARG));
        args.push(data_dir.as_os_str().to_owned());
        args
    }

Its unit test becomes (the data directory follows the kind of host):

    #[test]
    fn the_host_arguments_name_the_kind_of_host_and_the_data_directory() {
        let dir = std::path::Path::new("D:\\data");
        assert_eq!(rust_host_args(false, dir), ["office-com-host", "--data-dir", "D:\\data"].map(OsString::from));
        assert_eq!(rust_host_args(true, dir), ["office-com-host", "--session", "--data-dir", "D:\\data"].map(OsString::from));
    }

and host/mod.rs's unit test `the_session_flag_is_the_one_the_transport_passes` compares
`crate::route::rust_host_args(true, Path::new("d"))[..2]` with `[OsString::from("office-com-host"), OsString::from(SESSION_FLAG)]`.
tests/transport_route.rs: the four assertions that expect `["office-com-host", "--session"]` / `["office-com-host"]` (lines
for the docx session, the docx call and the two detect calls) expect the same list followed by `"--data-dir"` and the
transport's data directory (the `dir` its `office(...)` helper returns, as `to_string_lossy`).

## 6. mixdog-office-com/src/session.rs

(a) `use crate::sweep::Sweeper;` with the other crate imports.

(b) `pub(crate) struct Attach` gains `pub(crate) sweeper: Sweeper,`; `pub(crate) struct HostClient` gains `sweeper: Sweeper,`
and `HostClient::attach` stores it (`sweeper: how.sweeper,` beside `grace: how.grace`).

(c) `spawn_host`: start the host outside the worker's job, and fall back when the job does not allow it. Replace

    #[cfg(windows)]
    // CREATE_NO_WINDOW.
    command.creation_flags(0x0800_0000);
    let mut child = command.spawn()?;

with

    #[cfg(windows)]
    let mut child = {
        // CREATE_NO_WINDOW, and CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB: the ext-host worker's job kills what it holds when the worker
        // ends, and a host killed that way never runs the cleanup that closes its document and quits its application.
        // A job that does not allow breakaway refuses the flag (ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED): the host then starts inside the job,
        // and the sweep of the next start is its only protection.
        const CREATE_NO_WINDOW: u32 = 0x0800_0000;
        const CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB: u32 = 0x0100_0000;
        command.creation_flags(CREATE_NO_WINDOW | CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB);
        match command.spawn() {
            Ok(child) => child,
            Err(error) if error.raw_os_error() == Some(5) => {
                command.creation_flags(CREATE_NO_WINDOW);
                command.spawn()?
            }
            Err(error) => return Err(error),
        }
    };
    #[cfg(not(windows))]
    let mut child = command.spawn()?;

(d) `watch_exit` takes the sweeper: `async fn watch_exit(mut child, mut kill, readers, state, sweeper: Sweeper)`, the spawn in
`attach` passes `client.sweeper.clone()` (the field is set before the spawn), and right after
`state.send_modify(|state| state.exit = Some(code));` add

    // A host that left with a failure or a signal did not finish its cleanup: what it recorded is swept.
    if code != Some(0) {
        sweeper.request();
    }

(e) In `drain_once`, after `let clean_exit = ...;` add

    if !failures.is_empty() || !clean_exit {
        self.sweeper.request();
    }

(the host exited with code 0 but reported a failed cleanup on stderr, or exited with another code).

## 7. mixdog-office-com/src/host (the Rust host: what the session handler must do)

The Rust session and one-shot handlers are registered by later tasks (host/handlers.rs `registered()` still returns the
unregistered handlers), so the calls below are the contract this task adds for them; they are not edits of code that exists.

(a) host/mod.rs: `pub mod ledger;` beside `pub mod handlers;`. `run_with` reads `ledger::data_dir_from_args(args)` and hands it
to `HostEnv::new(lane, data_dir)`; `HostEnv` gains `data_dir: Option<PathBuf>` and `pub fn data_dir(&self) -> Option<&Path>`.
Without `--data-dir` (a hand-started host) there is no record and nothing else changes.

(b) A handler that owns an application builds its `Ledger` once: `Ledger::for_this_process(data_dir, &platform)`, and after every
change of what it knows of the application (the open that started it, a reopen that replaced it, a chart Excel that PowerPoint
started and the host tracked, the application's process exiting) calls
`ledger.record(env.lane(), &Entry { format, mode, ownership, owns_application, app_pid, app_start_ticks, chart_excel })`
(app_pid 0 with no chart Excel removes the file). `mode` is the session's `attach` | `visible` | `background` (`live` is already
`attach`); `app_start_ticks` is the value the handler already holds as `$state.AppStartTicks`; each tracked chart Excel carries
its own `Platform::process_start_ticks`. After the cleanup of the session (`close_session`'s cleanup, the end of the loop,
the one-shot `finally`) it calls `ledger.settle(env.lane(), cleanup_ok)` with the cleanup's `ok`: the file is deleted after a
cleanup with ok true and stays, for the sweep, otherwise. A failure to write or remove the record is not a failure of the
operation (ignore the `io::Result`).

(c) The PowerShell hosts (assets/*.ps1) write no record: they are the interim path and are deleted by
nr-office-comrs-retire-powershell. For them breakaway alone gives the cleanup-on-EOF guarantee Node had.

## 8. What this task does not cover

- A host that runs while the transport's process is killed too (the ext-host worker AND the daemon): the hosts still read EOF
  from their broken stdin pipe and clean up; the sweep covers a host that did not.
- The live end-to-end case "kill the host with TerminateProcess, the next start reaps the hidden WINWORD" needs the Rust
  session handler of nr-office-comrs-session-lifecycle to write the record (section 7); tests/containment_live.rs covers the
  sweep on a real hidden WINWORD and the breakaway on a worker stand-in until then.
